WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:06.030
HBO's sci-fi series Westworld takes us on
an intriguing journey into an artificially

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:11.549
intelligent future and a synthetic, reimagined
past, to a place where incredibly life-like

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:15.650
robots cater to the every whim of human guests
at an Old West theme park.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.860
Based on Michael Crichton's 1973 movie of
the same name, the show brings together a

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.920
fantastic cast including Anthony Hopkins,
Jeffrey Wright, and Evan Rachel Wood.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.970
So, let's take a look at 10 crazy facts about
HBO's Westworld.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:33.350
The fly that crawls across the face of Dolores
Abernathy in the show's premiere is actually

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:34.350
real!

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.399
In fact the production lists three fly wranglers
in its credits, and according to actress Evan

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:42.899
Rachel Wood, who plays Westworld's oldest
host, the flies used in the scene were semi-frozen,

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:44.230
so they were in a dormant state.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:47.920
Then when they were placed on her face, they
thawed and crawled around for ten minutes,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.140
until eventually they could fly away.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:56.250
However of course when we see the fly on her
eyeball, but was added later in post production.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:01.190
Apparently, Evan Rachel Wood got so Zen during
filming that she actually fell asleep while

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.970
she was shooting a scene with Jeffrey Wright,
who plays the head of Westworld's Programming

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.450
Division, Bernard Lowe!

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.390
It happened during one of the show's "Analysis
Mode" scenes, which are when the park's staff

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.550
question the hosts who aren't meant to remember
anything about it.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:18.340
Wood says that filming those scenes was almost
like meditating or going into a hypnotic state,

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.990
as she had to really concentrate on every
move she made because each slight move or

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.259
subtle facial expression would really affect
how Dolores would be interpreted both by the

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:31.200
other characters in the story and by the audience
watching the show at home.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.840
Although it's a rather adult theme park compared
to Disneyland, Westworld was actually inspired

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.450
by the Happiest Place on Earth!

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.790
Yes, when author Michael Crichton first saw
Disneyland's animatronics, it gave him the

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:48.130
idea to write and direct the 1970s movie Westworld,
which this new series draws on.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:52.570
Likewise, showrunners Jonathan Nolan and Lisa
Joy have designed their Westworld theme park

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:57.670
to be, as they see it, "a fantasy world […] compelling
enough to last generations", in other words,

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.460
it's a cultural institution much like Disneyland.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.490
Indeed, one of the inspirations behind the
show's character Dr Robert Ford, who founded

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.200
Westworld's park, is Walt Disney himself,
and not Jurassic Park founder John Hammond,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.580
as showrunner Lisa Joy says she's never seen
the dinosaur-sized blockbuster!

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.390
Which is funny because the character of John
Hammond was also created by author Michael

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.800
Crichton, and was likened to Walt Disney!

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.570
The production crew transported several big
interior sets from California to Castle Valley

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:30.110
and Dead Horse Point State Park in Utah where
they filmed a number of the show's exteriors.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:34.820
For example, they put the set for the train
we see arriving at Westworld on a flatbed

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.530
truck and drove it up and down Route 128 in
Utah.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.300
So, when we see James Marsden or Ed Harris's
characters looking out of the train window,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.290
they're actually looking out at the real Utah
landscape, and not a green screen.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.280
Showrunners Jonathan Nolan and Lisa Joy chose
Utah for filming Westworld as they wanted

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.090
the show and its theme park to have the feel
of the vast landscapes seen in director John

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.230
Ford's classic westerns, which also shot in
the state.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:00.730
The town at the heart of Westworld was filmed
at Melody Ranch in California's Santa Clarita

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.700
Valley, which was has also been home to many
productions from the 1950s Lone Ranger TV

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.760
series to Deadwood and the recent Magnificent
Seven remake.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.400
According to the website Deadline, extras
who appeared in Westworld were asked to sign

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.320
a pretty interesting consent form.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.810
The form included possible requirements the
extras might face on set, for example to 'have

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:23.530
[their] genitals painted', or to 'pose on
all fours while others who are fully nude

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.620
ride on [their] back', or even 'to contort
to form a table-like shape while being fully

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.620
nude'.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.751
The Hollywood website also said the extras
were paid around $600 for the shoot, which

00:03:32.751 --> 00:03:35.000
is nearly four times their usual day rate.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.760
HBO responded to the claims saying that the
"document […] was created by an outside

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.320
extras casting vendor" and that it "was not
requested, written or approved by HBO, Warner

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.230
Bros Television, or the producers."

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:51.010
Showrunners Jonathan Nolan and Lisa Joy did
a lot of research to create the theme park

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.010
in Westworld.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.950
This included visiting Las Vegas to study
how casinos are laid out and specifically

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:59.990
how the Strip is designed to lure visitors
into different experiences.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:04.700
Nolan and Joy also dug deep into the contemporary
world of Augmented Reality and explored the

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.302
rules under which Artificial Intelligence
is created and destroyed.

00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:12.290
In fact, some of the questions put to Westworld's
hosts, for example, whether they're lying,

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:17.660
are based on criteria used by real-life AI
researchers to decide if the AI they're creating

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.820
is problematic.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.980
The conversations Nolan and Joy had with people
in Silicon Valley about the current and future

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:27.150
state of AI were mostly off the record, so
they also decided to bring that notion of

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.120
extreme secrecy to their show.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.400
Video games such as Red Dead Redemption, Skyrim,
Grand Theft Auto, and a number of games by

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.370
BioWare also had a big impact on Westworld.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.950
While Jonathan Nolan is a long-time gamer,
his fellow showrunner Lisa Joy isn't, so to

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:46.650
fully understand the rules and components
of gameplay, which were crucial for their

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.690
show, they played a lot of Grand Theft Auto
together, however Joy's approach to the game

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.080
was rather unusual and included obeying traffic
signs rather than mowing people down!

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.660
When it came to writing their show, the realism,
immersion, violence, idea of morality as a

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.370
variable, and the level of sophistication
of non-player characters in contemporary gaming

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.480
all made their mark on Westworld's theme park.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.850
Movie-wise, Ridley Scott's Blade Runner was
also an influence on Westworld.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:16.620
In fact, Jonathan Nolan says he's watched
the 1982 classic sci-fi flick countless times

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.060
as it's his brother, Christopher's favourite
film.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.810
And while the look of Westworld's amusement
park draws on the classic Westerns of director

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.880
John Ford, for the control room scenes the
show's cinematographer Paul Cameron went for

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.080
a mix 'of the starkness of 2001: A Space Odyssey
or A Clockwork Orange for the upper floors'

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.220
and Slaughterhouse-Five for 'the bottom floor'.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:39.180
Nolan has also compared the amnesia programmed
into Westworld's hosts to the amnesia of Guy

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.460
Pearce's character in Memento, which was based
on Jonathan's short story and adapted for

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.680
the big screen by his brother Christopher.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.990
You can thank JJ Abrams, co-creator of Lost
and director of Star Wars: The Force Awakens,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.870
for getting the ball rolling on the new Westworld
series.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:58.639
Abrams came up with the idea to turn Michael
Crichton's original movie on its head and

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:02.780
focus the new series on the robot characters
and their perspective rather than just the

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.080
park's human guests.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.480
Funnily enough, this isn't the first time
Abrams has tried to get a new Westworld off

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:09.480
the ground.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.370
Over 20 years ago he sat down with Michael
Crichton, although things didn't work out.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:17.629
To bring the new Westworld to life, Abrams,
who was also a producer on Person of Interest,

00:06:17.629 --> 00:06:22.030
reached out to that show's creator Jonathan
Nolan, who's co-written movies such as Interstellar

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.870
and The Dark Knight with his brother Christopher.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.810
Westworld is the first collaboration between
Nolan and his wife, Burn Notice producer Lisa

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.720
Joy.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.139
The perforated paper rolls that spin through
the saloon's player piano, performing Old

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:40.460
Timey versions of songs by the likes of Radiohead
and The Rolling Stones, are real, fully-functioning

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.460
rolls created especially for the show by Stephen
Kent Goodman, whose California-based company

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:49.370
Gnaw-Vol-ty Rolls was given a super-tight
deadline to complete the highly specialised

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.370
work.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.850
Jonathan Nolan has said that the player piano
that features in Dr Robert Ford's workshop,

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:58.710
the saloon, and the title sequence is a shout-out
to author Kurt Vonnegut whose first novel

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:04.250
Player Piano was published in 1952 and takes
place in an almost fully mechanised near-future.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:08.010
And not only does the instrument serve as
a metaphor for Westworld's hosts, but the

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:12.970
fact that it plays modern music in an old-fashioned
style is also a reminder of just how artificial

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.440
and anachronistic the show's theme park is.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.910
Now, guys, what do you think of Westworld
so far?

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:21.090
And what are your theories about the world
the show's set in?

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:25.980
If you enjoyed this video, do comment, like
and hit that subscribe button.

